A timer inside a component that is built at run time gets its own schedule.

`Car` sits under a reactive `if`, so it survives lowering as a runtime instance
and html emits a `__cf_Car` factory for it. The timer written inside it has to
arrive as part of that factory: its handle is a local of the factory call, which
is what makes one per instance, and the effect machinery `passEffect` builds
goes there too.

What it must not do is survive as a node of its own. `passInlinePure` refused an
override holding a var, so the bare `timer` reached `passDeclarative`, which
turned it into `__cf_timer({ interval: 100 })` -- a factory nothing emits. The
first click threw `ReferenceError: __cf_timer is not defined` and took the whole
reactive branch with it. Before html's timer was an effect the same program
emitted no schedule at all and never ticked, silently, so no fixture noticed.

A var no rendered expression reads needs neither an updater nor a setter, which
is the whole of what a substitution cannot supply -- so it does not make the
override impure. The allowance is narrow on purpose: this build's platform
override only, and only where the position holds one copy of the body. A user
component with a private counter still becomes a runtime instance, and neither
is substituted under a `for`.

deny out/none/html/index.html `__cf_timer` -- the override inlines; no factory is emitted for it
deny out/none/html/index.html `var handle__` -- the handle is a factory local, not page state

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:time"
import . "sngl:app"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

component Car() ui {
    var n = 0

    timer(interval=100ms, enabled=true, @tick { n += 1 })
    text(value="n=" + string(n))
}

component main ui {
    var shown = false

    button(text="toggle", @click { shown = !shown })
    if shown {
        Car()
    }
}

window {
    main
}
